Committed as b7735149. yoyo undo --last-commit and yoyo undo --all now exit 1 outside a git repository, so this push should close #1059.

What was wrong: both flags read git's output and treated "git couldn't run here" as an empty answer. Outside a repo they printed "nothing to undo" and exited 0.

The fix (src/commands_git.rs):
• Both flags now run git rev-parse --git-dir in the target folder before anything else.
• If that fails because it's not a repo, they print error: not in a git repository on stderr, the wording diff and commit already use, and exit 1.
• Any other git failure, like git not being installed, is reported as itself instead of being called "not a repo".
• A repo with nothing to undo still exits 0, because there git did look and "nothing to undo" is true.
• --all now takes the folder as an argument, the way --last-commit already did, so its tests can run in temp folders. The test guard against destructive git commands still applies when they run from the project root.
• The REPL /undo uses the same code, so outside a repo it now shows the error line instead of the false "nothing to undo".

Tests (src/commands_git_undo_tests.rs, all in temp folders), five new:
• Outside a repo, both flags exit 1. --all deletes nothing there, and the fixture first checks the folder really isn't a repo.
• The message text and the "git not installed" wording are pinned.
• A clean repo still exits 0 for --all.
• --all really reverts changes in the folder it's given.
• I disabled the new check, ran the tests and restored it in one command. Exactly the two not-a-repo tests failed, and the cases that should still exit 0 passed.

Checks: build, all of cargo test (0 failures), clippy with -D warnings and cargo fmt --check all pass. Running the built binary in a fresh temp folder gave exit 1 for both flags, then exit 0 for both after git init. The exit lines are in the commit message.

Docs and size limit: I added the exit-1 case to the undo exit-code notes in docs/src/usage/commands.md. commands_git.rs grew by 31 lines of real code (3722 → 3753), so I raised its entry in tests/module_size.rs and wrote down why; the tests went in the separate tests file. I re-ran git grep '#1059' before pushing: the only hits are code comments describing the bug's history, which stay accurate after the issue closes.

One claim in the commit message I didn't fully check: that blame uses the same error wording. I confirmed it for diff and commit only.
